INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('8751d4686af0e7e91b01','AMEDEX 2025 NEUROLOGY (2).pdf','169','Neurology','Lindsay, 35 years of age, presents to your practice with a 48-hour history of facial weakness. She has no remarkable past medical history, is on no medications currently, and has been fit and well to date. On examination, she is asked to smile. Her appearance while smiling is shown in the accompanying photograph. She has no tinnitus, vertigo, or hearing loss. Otoscopic examination is normal. Which one of the following options is the most likely diagnosis?',NULL,'B','Option B Is correct

In the photograph, the forehead lines and
nasolabial fold on the right side are lost on smiling.
Also, the right mouth corner has failed to be
elevated while she is smiling. These features
suggest the complete right-sided facial palsy. With
no clue on exam pointing toward a specific
underlying cause, as well as the absence of vertigo
or hearing problems, Bell''s palsy (idiopathic facial
nerve palsy) is the most likely diagnosis.

Bell’s palsy is the most common cause of unilateral
facial paralysis. This idiopathic condition, is one of
the most common neurologic disorders of the
cranial nerves. In most patients, Bell palsy

gradually resolves over time.

Signs and symptoms of Bell palsy include the
following:

e Acute onset of unilateral upper and lower
facial paralysis (over a 48-hour period)

e Posterior auricular pain

e Decreased tearing

e Hyperacusis (increased hearing sensitivity)

e Taste disturbances (anterior 2/3 of the

tongue is innervated by the facial nerve)

e Otalgia (ear pain)
e Weakness of the facial muscles

e Poor evenacocute aw

Signs and symptoms of Bell palsy include the

following:

e Acute onset of unilateral upper and lower
facial paralysis (over a 48-hour period)

e Posterior auricular pain

e Decreased tearing

e Hyperacusis (increased hearing sensitivity)

e Taste disturbances (anterior 2/3 of the
tongue is innervated by the facial nerve)

e Otalgia (ear pain)

e Weakness of the facial muscles

e Poor eyelid closure

e Aching of the ear or mastoid

e Tingling or numbness of the cheek/mouth

e Excessive watering of the eye

¢ Ocular pain

e Blurred vision

e Flattening of forehead and nasolabial fold on
the side affected by palsy

e When patient raises eyebrows, palsy-
affected side of forehead remains flat

e When patient smiles, face becomes
distorted and lateralizes to side opposite the
palsy

(Option A) Acoustic neuroma presents with
hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo, none of which
Lindsay has. Acoustic neuromas, however, can
cause facial paralysis due to pressure on the facial
nerve. This occurs later in the course of the
.''sease. It is very unlikely that facial nerve pal

the first presentation of an acoustic neuroma.
